# Rules run over the WHOLE graph at the end of every indexing pass that changed something, and
# their conclusions replace `graph:derived` wholesale. Two consequences worth internalizing before
# adding a rule here:
#
#   1. DO NOT write transitive-closure rules for a relation a query can already walk. SPARQL 1.1
#      property paths do reachability natively and lazily: `deus:imports+` answers "everything this
#      file transitively imports" in ~0.2s over this repo, materializing nothing. The equivalent
#      closure rule
#
#          { ?a deus:dependsOn ?b. ?b deus:dependsOn ?c } => { ?a deus:dependsOn ?c }.
#
#      cost 147s per pass and 123,692 stored quads for the same answers. It was removed for exactly
#      that reason. Inverse (`^deus:imports`), alternative (`deus:imports|deus:importsModule`) and
#      bounded-length paths are all queries too.
#
#   1a. The reasoner is fed only the facts whose predicate a rule actually names (that narrowing is
#      dropped if any rule leaves a predicate unbound, `{ ?s ?p ?o }`). Naming your predicates keeps
#      the phase cheap: over this repo it is the difference between 30s and 4s per pass.
#
#   2. Rules earn their place where a query cannot go: recursion over a *pattern* rather than a
#      single predicate, joins that introduce new vocabulary, and conclusions other rules build on.
#      Keep the output small — it is recomputed from scratch every pass.
#
# The rule below is a linear scan with a handful of conclusions: which non-test files pull in a
# test file, which is a lint the import graph cannot express as a path.

@prefix deus: <https://dxos.org/vocab/deus#>.
@prefix string: <http://www.w3.org/2000/10/swap/string#>.

{
  ?importer deus:imports ?imported .
  ?imported deus:path ?path .
  ?path string:matches "^.*\\.(test|spec)\\.[tj]sx?$" .
  ?importer deus:path ?importerPath .
  ?importerPath string:notMatches "^.*\\.(test|spec)\\.[tj]sx?$" .
} => {
  ?importer deus:importsTestFile ?imported .
}.
